  "live_records": {
    "q-17605": {
      "dom_id": "step-q17605",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14227407/assets/a66ac2663dadacc0648b5a8993df40edafe8c7c2ee9dddac4ef932203b99dbd7.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the graph of $y=f(\\frac{1}{4}x)?$",
      "worked_solution": "To graph the function $y=f(\\frac{1}{4}x),$ we take the graph of $y=f(x)$ and stretch it by factor of $4$ parallel to the $x$-axis. This has the effect of multiplying the $x$-coordinates of all points on $y=f(x)$ by $4.$\n\nThe resulting graph is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14227407/assets/ca828042bdfd2b76c8fcd9ead928b8d5b13f7c03a0bdb7f3f7142cc880ecca04.png)",
