    "q-79427": {
      "dom_id": "step-q79427",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14227407/assets/75f479ff700f106f5cae6bafa94df87381d5fc0fca1758aad55f6bc5be1d7c38.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the graph of $y=f(\\frac{1}{4}x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)",
      "worked_solution": "To graph the function $y=f(\\frac{1}{4}x),$ we take the graph of $y=f(x)$ and stretch it by factor of $4$ parallel to the $x$-axis. This has the effect of multiplying the $x$-coordinates of all points on $y=f(x)$ by $4.$\n\nThe resulting graph is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14227407/assets/0f3cf395fe5f442212e057f73ef48ab458370e914424e186611c70f1c9f29953.png)",
